	</section>

	<section class="section container reveal">
		<Eyebrow>DILIGENCE</Eyebrow>
		<h2 class="iv-h2">We write the kill rule first, and follow it.</h2>
		<p class="iv-lede">
			A single-author result is easy to tune until it flatters. So each test gets its rule before
			the run, and the rule stays on the record when it goes against us.
		</p>
		<div class="iv-wait">
			<p class="iv-wait-lead">
				Three pre-set tests have gone against us. Each one changed what we build.
			</p>
			<p class="iv-wait-body">
				April 2026: the predecessor architecture lost to a plain CNN at all four noise levels of a
				purpose-built suite, and zero corrections fired in 42 runs. We rewrote the system. October
				2026: a pilot testing whether disagreement between parts flags mislabelled samples came back
				negative, so V2 moved to parallel parts, and the next pilot must beat plain loss by 0.03
				AUROC, published either way.
			</p>
			<p class="iv-wait-body">
				Also October 2026: the paper rule was not to submit if ORMAS finished two or more points below a plain reset. It finished 4.8 points below on one seed, so the paper was held, and V2 keeps only detect and reset. The product is the detector and the record.
			</p>
		</div>
		<div class="iv-assets">
			<p class="iv-assets-label">What goes in front of an investor</p>
			<ul class="iv-assets-list">
				<li>The deck: 14 slides and a 29-page appendix, grouped by the question an investor asks.</li>
				<li>Every number and its source, including the ones that are plans rather than measurements.</li>
				<li>The full results table, every run and baseline, weak runs included.</li>
				<li>The kill-rule record and the venue record.</li>
				<li>The three-year model, the acquisition plan, unit economics and the go-to-market budget, every row with its assumption.</li>
				<li>Personas, the competitor map by threat, five forces, and the entity and IP plan.</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="band band--surface section reveal">
		<div class="container">
			<Eyebrow>TERMS</Eyebrow>
			<h2 class="iv-h2">$750K on a post-money SAFE. $7.5M cap, 10.0% dilution.</h2>
			<p class="iv-lede">
				The cap sits below Carta’s median of about $10M for SAFEs of $250K–$1M, on purpose: a company
				with one founder, no paying customer and an evidence base confined to CIFAR should not price at
				the top of its category.
			</p>
			<p class="iv-note">
				Oxiedo is not yet incorporated, has no cap table and no prior investor. The Delaware
				C-corporation is formed before the SAFE is executed, and the code, run archive, paper, brand
				and domain are assigned to it at incorporation. Until then the founder holds them personally,
				and contracting runs through an existing UK-registered company, named in full in any agreement,
				which takes no equity. Founder stock vests. Bangladesh foreign-exchange rules on US shares go
				to counsel before close.
